WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.950
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Whether it's pizza, pie,

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.608
or anything else
round, you can't

00:00:10.608 --> 00:00:12.400
calculate the circumference
without knowing

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:15.023
about 3.14, also known as pi.

00:00:15.023 --> 00:00:16.690
But there's a few
more digits than that.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.190
So let's talk to Emma and see
how she broke the world record

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:22.400
for calculating 31.4 trillion
digits using Google Cloud

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:23.800
Platform.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.760
So, Emma, how did you do it?

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.330
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: So we ran
a program called y-cruncher

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.940
for four months on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.820
And we set up a
class of 25 machines

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.840
and we made sure that these
machines ran continuously,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.182
without any shutdown or
reboots for 121 days.

00:00:41.182 --> 00:00:42.140
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.630
I mean, that's no easy feat.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:45.820
120 days, all those
machines running

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.903
non-stop, that's kind of
something magical, right?

00:00:47.903 --> 00:00:48.862
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah.

00:00:48.862 --> 00:00:51.370
Well, Google Cloud has a world
of features and mechanisms

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.280
to make sure these machines and
software are up and running.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.360
And we sell the modem
systems and other techniques

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.225
that we use for our
production servers.

00:01:01.225 --> 00:01:03.100
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Because,
normally speaking,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.400
it's very difficult to keep one
process running over 121 days,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.250
nonetheless, on 24 machines.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.250
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Right.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.500
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
inspired you to kind of look

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.160
for this many digits of pi?

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.190
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Like
humanity, collectively,

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:18.300
tried to calculate as many
digits of pi as possible,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.770
since like 2000 BC.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.980
And even the first
electronic computer, ENIAC,

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.410
was used to calculate
2000 digits of pi.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.770
And ever since then, pi
was used as a benchmark

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.620
to measure the performance
and reliability of computers.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.440
And now we use
Google Cloud, so that

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.703
means Cloud is capable of doing
this kind of heavy duty work.

00:01:38.703 --> 00:01:40.370
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
That's awesome to see.

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.610
Congratulations on
breaking the world record.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.830
Of course, there's more
than just numbers in pi.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.750
We can also use it to make art.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.760
Let's talk to Mathias about
how he managed to do really,

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.930
really cool generative art
using all of Emma's work.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:53.430
Mathias--

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.160
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Hi.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Tell
me a little bit more

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:56.920
about what you've done here.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:58.003
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Right.

00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:00.760
So 31 trillion digits
is a huge number, right?

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:01.110
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Absolutely.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:01.330
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Right.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.130
But not only is it
a lot of numbers,

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.320
what's really interesting
about pi is also

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.150
that the sequence
never repeats itself.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:09.104
So we thought, what
could we do with this.

00:02:09.104 --> 00:02:10.479
So what we came
up with is, we've

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:13.870
made this algorithm that will
turn these sequences of pi

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.000
into a visual
representation, essentially.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.640
And this allows people
to find 31 trillion

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.178
different visual representations
of pi somewhere within pi.

00:02:23.178 --> 00:02:24.720
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Can we take a look?

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.012
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Absolutely.

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:27.880
So we can jump to
any digit within pi

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.800
that we want by just
typing in a random number.

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.810
So from that point on it takes
the next several thousand

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.300
digits of pi, them being
unique within there,

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.040
and then generates a piece of
art out of this, essentially.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if anyone
else is here, they can come by,

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.772
they can change up the
numbers, and they'll

00:02:43.772 --> 00:02:45.490
see different
artwork because it's

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.615
unique sets of pi patterns.

00:02:46.615 --> 00:02:47.782
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Exactly.

00:02:47.782 --> 00:02:50.380
So someone could come by,
say they type in their phone

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.510
numbers, or like maybe
their birthday, the birthday

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.550
of their spouse, or whatnot.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.470
And type this in
and then essentially

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.403
get a unique representation
of that number within pi.

00:02:59.403 --> 00:03:00.820
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Very, very cool.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.695
This is just one of the
many cool experiments

00:03:02.695 --> 00:03:04.300
that we have here
at the DevZone.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:07.010
So hopefully the people here can
come check it out and see all

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:08.260
the cool work that we've done.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:11.310
[MUSIC PLAYING]

